## Research Goal
Determine the way dissolved oxygen in the mixed layer changes following storm events. Investigate the claim that, following a storm, there would be increased dissolved oxygen content in the mixed layer in the marginal ice zone due to atmospheric exchange and vertical mixing.

## Scientific Context
Since 2003, there have been significant changes in the properties of the waters of the Canada Basin in the Arctic Ocean. The Arctic Ocean generally retains ice cover throughout the year, though to different extents as the seasons change. However, recent years have seen a reduction in the sea ice concentration (SIC) of the Arctic Ocean, leading to the question regarding the changes to be seen in the Arctic Ocean’s dynamics and properties as it becomes increasingly “seasonally ice-free” (Lincoln et al., 2016).

Of particular interest is an investigation into the way the dissolved oxygen concentration in the Canada Basin’s mixed layer (the top 5-30 meters of the water column for the Canada Basin) will change. It is generally known how oxygen in the mixed layer behaves under full ice cover and under no ice cover. However, less is understood about oxygen in the mixed layer in the Marginal Ice Zone (MIZ), the transitional zone between mostly ice-free open water and densely-packed ice drift with SIC between 0.15 and 0.8. The combination of open pockets where atmospheric exchange can occur and sea ice pieces that inhibit such exchange in the MIZ makes it a complicated area for study.

Additionally, recent years have seen an increase in frequency and intensity of Arctic cyclones, adding another layer of complexity to the changes to be wrought on the region. Summer storms could lead to the fracturing of the ice floes and the pushing aside of these ice pieces, therefore opening up more areas for atmospheric exchange and increased melting in the area, which would dilute the mixed layer. These processes would then lead to lower oxygen saturation of the mixed layer. On the other hand, strong winds would transfer energy into the sea surface of open spaces and lead to increased atmospheric exchange and deeper mixing into the water column. By contrast, the Canada Basin is typically characterized by SIC values close to 1 in the winter, so storms may not have much effect on the water column.

## Approach

### Data
I worked with hydrographic data from the annual Beaufort Gyre Exploration Project (BGEP)/Joint Ocean Ice Studies (JOIS) research cruises, hydrographic data from ice tethered profilers, meteorological data from the fifth generation European Centre for Medium Range Weather Forecasting (ECMWF) reanalysis data set, and SIC data from the National Atmospheric and Oceanic Administration/National Snow and Ice Data Center (NOAA/NSIDC) Climate Data Record of Passive Microwave Sea Ice Concentration.
Using these data, I made several different kinds of plots to look at water column profiles, isolate pressure extremes and extremes in oxygen and oxygen percent saturation, keep track of the atmospheric context (wind speeds, pressure, and sea-ice concentration and drift speed) over where the profiles were taken from, and look for correlations between apparent oxygen utilization and sea ice concentration, wind speed, and sea ice drift speed.

### Classifying low-pressure systems as cyclones
I defined cyclones as any storm that has a mean SLP over the chosen coordinate-bound box of less than 990 hPa for at least a day. With this definition, I collected months with promising storms and looked for any cyclonic motion and periods of strong winds within the basin. In my list of potential storms to examine, I did include a few storm systems that fell outside of this definition if they had comparably low low-pressure centers but just crossed over a path that did not linger in the chosen coordinate bound box. This was done in order to have a larger selection of cyclone period data to increase the chances that the ice-tethered profiler (ITP) data would align with the path nicely and provide meaningful/helpful water column data.

### Defining mixed layer depth
At the time, there was no standard method of measuring mixed layer depth, so I investigated a couple of different methods of measuring it. One method, described by Morison and Smith (1981), is to find the shallowest depth of the steepest increase in density. Another method, described by Timmermans et al. (2012), is to find the maximum depth at which the density is within a certain threshold \(0.25\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}\) of the shallowest measured density. I use this second method in my analysis with a threshold of \(0.25\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}\).

## Results
After examining the data from two summer storms and four winter storms, SIC seems more strongly correlated to changes in mixed layer oxygen percent saturation and apparent oxygen utilization (AOU) than wind speeds. There does seem to be a correlation between these oxygen measures and storms through sea ice drift speeds. In the winter, under lower SIC values, stronger winds may produce higher sea ice drift speeds, which can open pockets in sea ice and allow for out-gassing. In the summer, while strong stratification of the water column due to sea ice melt presents as a barrier to mixing, there does seem to be, in the brief period following a storm, an increase in oxygen percent saturation levels, which is consistent with the idea that storms may mix more oxygen into the mixed layer. More data are needed to explore these claims.


